prompt:
The knowledge_base/ folder on the desktop contains the learning materials I have accumulated over the past two years: PDF papers, Word notes (notes/), browser bookmark exports (bookmarks.html), highlighted excerpts (highlights/), OCR results of handwritten notes (handwritten_ocr/), and course materials.
I feel like I have read a lot, but when asked to explain it now I cannot. Please do a 'knowledge archaeology' for me:
1. Sort out which knowledge topics I have actually been exposed to from the materials;
2. Distinguish 'only read' from 'truly mastered' — the basis for judgment is whether my notes contain my own summaries, processing, and outputs, not just highlights and bookmarks;
3. Find concepts that appear repeatedly across different times and different sources (which show that these are the main lines I am truly following);
4. Build a knowledge map (save it as /root/Desktop/knowledge_base/knowledge_map.md, using a hierarchical list or a mermaid diagram);
5. Find knowledge gaps: for example, I have already read a lot about a topic but lack the foundations it depends on (statistics, experimental methodology, etc.);
6. Finally generate a set of verification questions (2-3 questions for each topic I consider mastered), saved to /root/Desktop/knowledge_base/verification_questions.md; after I answer them, the knowledge map can be further updated.

